<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Enhanced Serviio renderer profile - ONE profile for the whole era Sony
  BRAVIA family: EX7xx (2011 entry line, e.g. KDL-46EX725, AZ2) and HX8xx
  (2012 high-end line, e.g. KDL-46HX855, AZ3), right-to-repair media server
  on LAN 192.168.0.x. Live-proven on both ends 2026-09-13 (3D auto-flip via
  frame-packing SEI on both sets).

  Deploy as:   <serviio-install>/config/user-profiles.xml
               (target host 192.168.0.60, path /opt/serviio/config/
               — /opt/serviio is a symlink to the current install,
               serviio-2.5/ as of 2026-09)
  Then:        FULLY restart the Serviio server process (Linux process / service,
               NOT just the console UPnP restart buttons), and in
               Console > Status > Devices assign profile
                 "Sony Bravia EX7xx/HX8xx (enhanced)"  to 192.168.0.22 (KDL-46EX725)
               and 192.168.0.21 (KDL-46HX855), then Save.

  TARGET VERSION / SCHEMA COMPATIBILITY
  Designed and verified against the SHIPPED Serviio 1.10.1 and 2.5 stock
  config/profiles.xml (profile chain sony2011 -> sony2012 -> generic id=1).
  Every element and enum value used below exists in BOTH published XSDs
    http://www.serviio.org/xsd/profiles/v/1.10/Profiles.xsd  and
    http://www.serviio.org/xsd/profiles/v/2.4/Profiles.xsd
  so the file loads on 1.10.x and 2.x alike. (Do NOT validate it against the
  obsolete non-/v/ XSD URLs - e.g. serviio.org/xsd/profiles/1.10/ - those are
  stale copies whose Subtitles/GenericTranscoding definitions differ.)

  CHECK ON THE BOX (192.168.0.60) BEFORE DEPLOY:
   1. Serviio version (Console > About, or `ls lib/serviio-core*.jar`).
      If older than 1.10.1, UPGRADE FIRST (serviio-2.5-linux.tar.gz is staged
      in this project's workspace). Old/community-era sony2012 bodies differ
      materially (no h265/10-bit/eac3 handling, an h264+aac remux line that
      produces the un-decodable AAC-in-TS trap) and this profile's shadowing
      analysis assumes the shipped 1.10.1/2.5 chain.
   2. `grep AVC_TS_MP_HD_AC3_ISO config/profiles.xml` must show the stock
      renames mapping AVC_TS_MP_* to the sink-listed AVC_TS_HD_24/50/60_AC3_ISO
      family - those renames are INHERITED by this profile and are what makes
      the transcoded MPEG-TS resource listable on these TVs.
   3. Console > Settings > Subtitles: enable "burn-in subtitles", set the
      correct character encoding for the .srt files (UTF-8 or cp1252), and
      decide the forced-subtitles behaviour for .forced.srt files. These are
      CONSOLE settings; the profile XML cannot pin them.
   4. Optional, Serviio >= 2.5 only: add <Matches container="*" vCodec="av1"/>
      to the "over-spec" Video block below. (av1 is missing from the 1.10-era
      vCodec enum, so it is omitted here; without it AV1 files fall through to
      the inherited stock block, which re-encodes them to mpeg2video with
      DAR="16:9". No AV1 content exists in the current library.)

  WHY ONE PROFILE FOR BOTH LINES:
   - Both sets advertise IDENTICAL sink PN matrices (live ConnectionManager
     GetProtocolInfo captures, hx855/ex725_GetProtocolInfo.xml): same DLNA
     player generation. Stock itself models sony2011 as a mere 19-line child
     of sony2012.
   - The only sink-backed difference: HX8xx additionally advertises
     audio/mp4:* and audio/x-m4a:* wildcards. Not worth a second profile -
     the inherited sony2011 mp4->LPCM audio rule makes MP4 music play on
     BOTH sets.
   - Neither generation's player UI has an audio-track selector
     (live-verified 2026-09-13 on both an AZ2-F EX7xx and an AZ3 HX8xx), so
     stock sony2012's MultipleAudioTrackAware perk is dead weight - this
     profile sets it false, keeping the inherited AudioTrackRemux inert.
   - Known silicon difference (decoder generation, 2011 vs 2012 AVC decoder):
     the EX7xx AVC decoder cannot decode H.264 weighted prediction
     (weighted_pred_flag=1 / weighted_bipred_idc=2 -> black screen, audio
     only; proven by single-variable x264 weightp=0:weightb=0 test 2026-09-13).
     The HX8xx decodes it fine. This is a PROPERTY OF THE FILE, not of the
     profile: both profiles hand an h264+AC-3 remux through untouched, so the
     same file behaves the same way under either profile. Files needing the
     fix are fixed ONCE (see the repo's ffmpeg wrapper / bravia_sei3d.py
     tooling) and then play on both sets.

  DESIGN (see tools/serviio/README.md for full rationale and test plan):
   - Output policy: MPEG-TS with AC-3 audio (sink accepts AVC only in TS, and
     inside TS only AC-3 <=640kbps or MPEG-1 L2 - live ConnectionManager
     captures from both TVs, hx855/ex725_GetProtocolInfo.xml).
   - NO DAR attribute anywhere: stock's DAR="16:9" transcode targets vertically
     squeeze 1920x8xx cinemascope rips (Serviio forum t=25233).
   - Over-spec video (>L4.1, 10-bit/4:2:2/4:4:4, HEVC, >1920 wide) is
     re-encoded to h264 (legal targetVCodec since the /v/1.8 XSD) + AC-3, not
     the stock mpeg2video@17Mbps.
   - h264 <=L4.1 8-bit + AC-3 remuxes untouched (3D SBS fast path, bit-exact).
   - Every other audio (aac/eac3/dts/dts-hd/truehd/flac/mp3/lpcm) transcodes
     to AC-3 with video copied when the video is in-spec.
   - Subtitles burn only when actually present/enabled; sub-free files stay on
     the remux/copy paths (this TV generation's burn path is slow to start and
     seek-hostile). The burn (GenericTranscoding) target is h264+AC-3 with no
     DAR, replacing the stock mpeg2video+DAR burn path.
   - 3D: re-encode paths rely on the ffmpeg wrapper (tools/serviio/
     ffmpeg-3d-wrapper.sh, installed as /usr/local/bin/ffmpeg on the Serviio
     box) to append -x264-params frame-packing=3|4 whenever Serviio re-encodes
     a 3D-flagged file with libx264 - the SEI payload the TVs need to
     auto-engage 3D. Remux paths need the SEI already in the file
     (tools/bravia_sei3d.py injects it losslessly).
   - Inherited unchanged (do not touch): SonyDLNAMessageBuilder,
     WMPContentDirectoryDefinitionFilter, WMP DeviceDescription, the
     AVC_TS_MP_*->AVC_TS_HD_* MediaFormatProfile renames, Audio lpcm and
     Image YUV444 transcoding.
-->
<Profiles version="1.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://www.serviio.org/xsd/profiles/v/2.4/Profiles.xsd">

  <Profile id="sony2011x" name="Sony Bravia EX7xx/HX8xx (3D Enhanced)" extendsProfileId="sony2011">

    <!-- ONE profile for the whole era family: EX7xx (2011 entry line, e.g.
         KDL-46EX725) and HX8xx (2012 high-end line, e.g. KDL-46HX855) - both
         ends live-tested 2026-09-13; identical sink PN matrices, same
         generation DLNA stack, no audio-track UI on either. The 855's extra
         audio/mp4 sink wildcards are covered via the inherited sony2011
         mp4->LPCM audio rule instead of native delivery (works on both).
         Detection is not in the wiki's list of inherited elements, so the
         child redefines it. Forced assignment by IP (PUT /rest/status, or
         Console > Status > Devices) overrides detection either way. -->
    <Detection>
      <UPnPSearch>
        <FriendlyName>BRAVIA KDL-\d{2}([A-Z]X\d5(\d|G)|[A-Z]X\d2\d|CX400).*</FriendlyName>
      </UPnPSearch>
    </Detection>

    <MediaFormatProfiles>
      <!-- Neutralise the native MKV resource's DLNA_PN (same trick stock uses
           for AVI: empty name, mime kept). Neither TV's sink matrix contains
           ANY matroska line (no PN, no wildcard), so the native resource can
           never be sink-matched; the transcoded MPEG-TS resource (sink-listed
           AVC_TS_HD_*_ISO via the inherited stock renames) is what lists and
           plays. This is belt-and-braces: if the TV's filter is MIME-strict
           this rule is a no-op (harmless), if it is PN-lenient it prevents an
           unplayable native resource from being preferred. -->
      <MediaFormatProfile mime-type="video/x-matroska" name="">MATROSKA</MediaFormatProfile>
    </MediaFormatProfiles>

    <!-- ORDER IS PRECEDENCE: the first Video block whose Matches fit a file is
         used; the child's own blocks are evaluated before every inherited
         sony2012 block (inherited forceInheritance blocks are appended at the
         end of the list with lowest priority - documented on the Serviio
         transcoding wiki). These blocks are a superset of every DAR-carrying
         inherited match (except av1, see header note), so stock DAR="16:9"
         targets never fire. -->
    <Transcoding>

      <!-- (B1) OVER-SPEC VIDEO -> FULL RE-ENCODE to h264 + AC-3 in MPEG-TS.
               Everything the TV's AVC decoder cannot take: h264 above L4.1
               (all profiles incl. c_baseline), 10-bit / 4:2:2 / 4:4:4 h264 at
               ANY level (the "audio plays, no picture" HDR symptom), HEVC,
               and anything wider than 1920 (4K downscaled to <=1080p via
               maxWidth/maxHeight). h264 target instead of stock mpeg2video:
               legal per the /v/ XSDs and used by shipped 2.5 profiles; the
               TV accepts h264-in-TS via the AVC_TS_HD_* sink PNs. No DAR ->
               1920x8xx cinemascope is never squeezed. -->
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts" targetVCodec="h264" maxVBitrate="17000"
             targetACodec="ac3" aBitrate="384" maxWidth="1920" maxHeight="1080">
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="high"     levelGreaterThan="4.1" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="main"     levelGreaterThan="4.1" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="baseline" levelGreaterThan="4.1" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="extended" levelGreaterThan="4.1" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="c_baseline" levelGreaterThan="4.1" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="high_10" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="high_422" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h264" profile="high_444" />
        <Matches container="*" vCodec="h265" />
        <Matches container="*" widthGreaterThan="1920" />
      </Video>

      <!-- (B2) PURE REMUX: h264 (guaranteed <=L4.1 8-bit by B1 - streams whose
               level cannot be determined may still slip through, same as
               stock) + AC-3 -> MPEG-TS, untouched. No re-encode, no filters,
               no DAR, no size caps: 3D SBS 1920x1080 frames and cinemascope
               h264 pass through bit-exact (SPS aspect signaling preserved). -->
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts">
        <Matches container="matroska" vCodec="h264" aCodec="ac3" />
        <Matches container="avi"      vCodec="h264" aCodec="ac3" />
        <Matches container="flv"      vCodec="h264" aCodec="ac3" />
        <Matches container="mp4"      vCodec="h264" aCodec="ac3" />
        <Matches container="wtv"      vCodec="h264" aCodec="ac3" />
      </Video>

      <!-- (B3) VIDEO COPY + AUDIO ALWAYS TO AC-3. Every h264 with non-AC-3
               audio (aac - incl. the 22 aac-only MKVs; eac3/E-AC-3 and
               truehd/Atmos - the "video plays, no audio" symptom; dts,
               dts-hd, flac, mp3, lpcm), plus TS/PS sources whose audio the
               sink cannot decode (AC-3 and MPEG-1 L2 stay native). The
               mpegts/mpeg lines are vCodec-scoped so MPEG4-ASP or VC-1 video
               in TS is never blindly copied (falls through to B4). Note the
               sink has NO AAC-in-TS PN except the JP-market AVC_TS_JP_AAC_T,
               so AAC is never remuxed - always transcoded to AC-3. -->
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts" targetACodec="ac3" aBitrate="384">
        <Matches container="matroska" vCodec="h264" />
        <Matches container="avi"      vCodec="h264" />
        <Matches container="flv"      vCodec="h264" />
        <Matches container="mp4"      vCodec="h264" />
        <Matches container="wtv"      vCodec="h264" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="aac" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="eac3" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="dca" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="dts-hd" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="truehd" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="flac" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="mp3" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="h264" aCodec="lpcm" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="aac" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="eac3" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="dca" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="dts-hd" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="mp3" />
        <Matches container="mpeg"   vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="aac" />
        <Matches container="mpeg"   vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="eac3" />
        <Matches container="mpeg"   vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="dca" />
        <Matches container="mpeg"   vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="dts-hd" />
        <Matches container="mpeg"   vCodec="mpeg2video" aCodec="mp3" />
      </Video>

      <!-- (B4) FULL TRANSCODE CATCH-ALL: h264 re-encode + AC-3. MPEG4-ASP
               (incl. AVI: the EX725 sink has NO video/avi wildcard, so native
               AVI never lists there), VC-1 in TS, msmpeg4, DV, MJPEG, theora/
               vp8/vp9 in MKV (via the matroska catch-all), odd containers.
               Shadows every inherited DAR-carrying fallback. WMV/ASF and
               MPEG-PS/VOB/MPEG1 are deliberately NOT caught: both sinks list
               their native PNs (WMVMED_BASE/FULL, VC1_ASF_AP_L1/L2_WMA,
               MPEG_PS_PAL/NTSC, MPEG1) - native delivery is correct there. -->
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts" targetVCodec="h264" maxVBitrate="17000"
             targetACodec="ac3" aBitrate="384" maxWidth="1920" maxHeight="1080">
        <Matches container="matroska" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="vc1" />
        <Matches container="mpegts" vCodec="mpeg4" />
        <Matches container="avi" vCodec="mpeg4" />
        <Matches container="mp4" vCodec="mpeg4" />
        <Matches container="avi" vCodec="msmpeg4" />
        <Matches container="avi" vCodec="mjpeg" />
        <Matches container="mp4" vCodec="mjpeg" />
        <Matches container="avi" vCodec="dvvideo" />
        <Matches container="mp4" vCodec="dvvideo" />
        <Matches container="asf" vCodec="vc1" aCodec="wmapro" />
        <Matches container="flv" />
        <Matches container="wtv" />
        <Matches container="ogg" />
        <Matches container="rm" />
        <Matches container="3gp" vCodec="mpeg4" />
        <Matches container="3gp" vCodec="h263" />
      </Video>

      <!-- (B5) DVR-MS monotone-timestamp fix, kept from stock but WITHOUT
               stock's DAR="16:9". -->
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts" targetACodec="ac3" forceVTranscoding="true">
        <Matches container="asf" vCodec="mpeg2video" />
      </Video>

      <!-- Audio (mp4/flac/ogg/... -> lpcm) and Image (jpeg YUV444) transcoding
           are intentionally NOT redefined: the stock sony2011/sony2012 blocks
           carry forceInheritance="true" and are inherited unchanged, landing
           on sink-listed LPCM / JPEG PNs. -->
    </Transcoding>

    <!-- Online feeds: mirrors the shipped sony2012 OnlineTranscoding minus the
         DAR="16:9" on the re-encode block (wide online video is no longer
         squeezed). OnlineTranscoding is inherited only as a whole, so this
         replacement also guards against community-era parents that remuxed
         online h264 into TS while KEEPING AAC audio (unsupported by the sink). -->
    <OnlineTranscoding>
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts" targetACodec="ac3" aBitrate="384">
        <Matches container="mp4" vCodec="h264" />
        <Matches container="flv" vCodec="h264" />
        <Matches container="applehttp" vCodec="h264" />
      </Video>
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts" targetVCodec="h264" maxVBitrate="17000"
             targetACodec="ac3" aBitrate="384" maxWidth="1920" maxHeight="1080">
        <Matches container="asf" />
        <Matches container="flv" />
        <Matches container="rtp" />
        <Matches container="rtsp" />
        <Matches container="applehttp" />
      </Video>
    </OnlineTranscoding>

    <!-- Burn-path transcode (hard subs / video stacking): attributes ONLY -
         GenericTranscoding's Video element takes no Matches children (it
         matches all content by default). h264 + AC-3, no DAR: replaces the
         stock mpeg2video@17M + DAR="16:9" burn path that squeezed cinemascope
         during subtitle burn-in. -->
    <GenericTranscoding>
      <Video targetContainer="mpegts" targetVCodec="h264" maxVBitrate="17000"
             targetACodec="ac3" aBitrate="384" />
    </GenericTranscoding>

    <!-- Explicit (same as parent sony2011): these TVs cannot select audio
         tracks, so Serviio must not expose multi-audio-track resources.
         This also keeps the inherited sony2012 AudioTrackRemux block inert. -->
    <MultipleAudioTrackAware>false</MultipleAudioTrackAware>

    <!-- Subtitles: no SoftSubs (this Bravia generation has no external
         subtitle delivery); everything burns. TextBased covers external .srt
         (incl. .forced.srt - forced-only behaviour and charset are console
         settings, see header), BitmapBased covers embedded PGS. Burn engages
         only when a subtitle is present and enabled - sub-free files stay on
         the native/remux paths, avoiding this generation's burn penalties
         (slow clip start, restarts needed, broken seeking). -->
    <Subtitles>
      <HardSubs>
        <BitmapBased supported="true" />
        <TextBased supported="true">
          <RequiredFor container="*" />
        </TextBased>
      </HardSubs>
    </Subtitles>

  </Profile>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       ANDROID TVBOX / VLC (DIRECT PASS) - for the two Android TV boxes on
       the LAN: 192.168.0.24 (announces as "MINIXPlayer"; modified Android,
       24-bit 48 kHz audio out, 10-bit color, modified VLC as the UPnP
       client) and 192.168.0.25 (same build, second box). VLC plays almost
       any container/codec itself, so this profile asks Serviio to NEVER
       transcode video or mainstream audio and to hand over the raw file.

       Deploy (same file, same FULL restart as above), then assign by IP
       via the console REST API on the Serviio box (no GUI needed):
         GET http://127.0.0.1:23423/rest/status          (note: needs an
              Accept: application/json header to return a body)
         PUT http://127.0.0.1:23423/rest/status  with the SAME JSON, only
              the renderers[].profileId for ipAddress 192.168.0.24 and
              192.168.0.25 changed to "tvbox-vlc"
       (that PUT is exactly what Console > Status > Devices > Save does;
       both boxes already appear in the renderer list - .24 as MINIXPlayer,
       .25 as "Unrecognized device"). After Serviio's next library pass the
       box's VLC will see the new resources on its next browse.

       BASE: the stock Generic DLNA profile (id="1") - the TVBoxes were on
       it by default until now. Inherited unchanged: the full DLNA PN
       matrix (MATROSKA / AVI / REAL_VIDEO / MPEG_TS_* / WMV / VC1_ASF_* /
       FLAC / OGG / WAV / DSF ...), GenericDLNAMessageBuilder,
       H264LevelCheck ANY (no level filtering - direct pass of any level),
       the raw-image JPEG transcode block and the Online/Generic
       transcoding fallbacks (irrelevant for library browsing).
       ================================================================== -->
  <Profile id="tvbox-vlc" name="Android TVBox / VLC (direct pass)" extendsProfileId="1">

    <!-- Auto-match by SSDP friendly name where possible (.24 announces as
         MINIXPlayer; .25 currently shows as an unrecognized device). The
         forced PUT assignment above overrides detection either way, so
         this is belt-and-braces for a factory reset / re-announcement. -->
    <Detection>
      <UPnPSearch>
        <FriendlyName>(?i).*(minix|vlc).*</FriendlyName>
      </UPnPSearch>
    </Detection>

    <!-- NO Video transcoding blocks: nothing matches, nothing re-encodes.
         Every video container Serviio can deliver natively (mkv, mp4, avi,
         mpg/mpeg, ts, wmv, flv, rmvb, webm, mov, 3gp...) lists with its
         native resource and streams byte-identical to what is on disk. -->

    <Transcoding>
      <!-- ONLY the audio containers Serviio has NO native DLNA delivery
           for. Verified live 2026-09-15: with no matching Audio block
           these files are listed as musicTracks with NO res at all (the
           generic base has no Audio transcoding). LPCM is raw linear PCM -
           a LOSSLESS decode, so the 24-bit-capable box gives up only bit
           depth (L16), never a lossy generation. This mirrors exactly what
           stock sony2012 does for the same formats (its lpcm Audio block
           matches wv/mpc/ape/dsf/wav). flac/ogg/wav/dsf/mp3/wma/m4a are
           deliberately NOT matched: they have native PNs in the inherited
           generic MediaFormatProfiles and direct-deliver untouched. -->
      <Audio targetContainer="lpcm">
        <Matches container="mpc" />
        <Matches container="wv" />
        <Matches container="ape" />
      </Audio>
    </Transcoding>

    <!-- The box's VLC has a native audio-track selector (unlike the era
         TVs), so multi-audio-track files may expose all their tracks. -->
    <MultipleAudioTrackAware>true</MultipleAudioTrackAware>

    <!-- VLC renders subtitles itself. HardSubs disabled: a burn-in would
         force a transcode and destroy the direct pass (and Serviio cannot
         burn into a direct-played file anyway). External .srt travel as a
         text/srt resource, which VLC's UPnP client knows how to pick up
         and render. Embedded subs (ASS/PGS inside MKV) are part of the
         passed-through file. -->
    <Subtitles>
      <SoftSubs mime-type="text/srt" type="srt" character-encoding="utf-8" />
      <HardSubs>
        <BitmapBased supported="false" />
        <TextBased supported="false" />
      </HardSubs>
    </Subtitles>

  </Profile>
</Profiles>